WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.892
The payout program keeps every payment it makes to a shop in a table called payouts: rows and columns, like a spreadsheet.

2
00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:13.712
Its amount column holds euros, like 40.00.

3
00:00:13.712 --> 00:00:20.896
You want cents instead, 4000, in a column called amount_minor, like every other money field in the company.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.273
The program runs as six copies, called instances.

5
00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:31.686
An update replaces them one at a time, so for a while old and new versions run side by side, on the same table.

6
00:00:31.686 --> 00:00:34.759
How do you change a column under both of them?

7
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:43.231
The quick way is one migration, a script that changes the table, shipped with the update: rename the column, convert it, done.

8
00:00:43.231 --> 00:00:44.483
Let's watch it run.

9
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:50.692
The moment it runs, every old instance still in the fleet fails: column amount does not exist.

10
00:00:50.692 --> 00:00:53.576
Until the rollout ends, those requests break.

11
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.598
Stop the program first, then?

12
00:00:55.598 --> 00:00:59.584
That avoids the errors, but the downtime grows with the table.

13
00:00:59.584 --> 00:01:07.684
Stripe, a large payments company, worked out that moving 100 million records at one a second would take more than three years.

14
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.282
Nobody's customers wait three years.

15
00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:14.643
What we want is a change that never surprises anyone, old or new.

16
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:17.146
Think about replacing an old bridge.

17
00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:21.449
Nobody blows it up on a Monday and hopes the drivers read the news.

18
00:01:21.449 --> 00:01:23.762
You build the new bridge next to it.

19
00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:25.496
For a while, both are open.

20
00:01:25.496 --> 00:01:27.744
Traffic moves across, lane by lane.

21
00:01:27.744 --> 00:01:31.791
And only when nobody drives over the old one, you take it down.

22
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:32.430
Map it.

23
00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.813
The old bridge is the amount column.

24
00:01:34.813 --> 00:01:36.864
The new bridge is amount_minor.

25
00:01:36.864 --> 00:01:41.299
The drivers are the instances, and some still have last year's map.

26
00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:44.608
Taking the old bridge down is dropping the column.

27
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:46.865
The picture breaks in one place.

28
00:01:46.865 --> 00:01:49.750
A bridge carries cars, it doesn't hold them.

29
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.536
Our columns hold values, so the old values also have to be copied across.

30
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:56.421
Step one: expand.

31
00:01:56.421 --> 00:01:59.145
Add amount_minor next to amount, empty.

32
00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:02.848
Old instances don't mention it, so they keep working.

33
00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.997
Step two: deploy version 2.

34
00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:08.358
It writes both columns, and still reads amount.

35
00:02:08.358 --> 00:02:13.007
Version 1 and version 2 now run side by side, and both are happy.

36
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.731
Step three: backfill.

37
00:02:14.731 --> 00:02:24.487
Once the last version 1 instance is gone, copy amount times 100 into amount_minor, a few thousand rows at a time, in small batches.

38
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:27.473
Why wait for the last version 1 to be gone?

39
00:02:27.473 --> 00:02:28.529
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:39.558
Because a version 1 instance can still change amount on a row version 2 just wrote, and it never touches amount_minor.

41
00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:41.340
That row is set, but wrong.

42
00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.632
So the backfill also fixes rows that differ, not only empty ones.

43
00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:49.222
Step four: deploy version 3, which reads amount_minor.

44
00:02:49.222 --> 00:02:53.510
To be careful, it reads both for a while and logs every difference.

45
00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.301
That's called a shadow read.

46
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:58.729
Step five: version 4 writes only amount_minor.

47
00:02:58.729 --> 00:03:00.076
Step six: contract.

48
00:03:00.076 --> 00:03:03.551
When no running version mentions amount, drop it.

49
00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:07.555
That's expand and contract, also called parallel change.

50
00:03:07.555 --> 00:03:14.668
Expand the schema to hold both shapes, move readers and writers across one rollout at a time, then contract it.

51
00:03:14.668 --> 00:03:20.435
And the rule behind every step: each step must work with the versions on both sides of it.

52
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.122
One hazard remains.

53
00:03:22.122 --> 00:03:26.751
Even an instant change to a table takes the strongest lock for a moment.

54
00:03:26.751 --> 00:03:31.959
If someone left a transaction open, a BEGIN with no end, the change waits for it.

55
00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:35.881
And every read that arrives after it waits behind the change.

56
00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:39.814
A one-millisecond change can stop every read of the table.

57
00:03:39.814 --> 00:03:43.855
So set a lock timeout of a few seconds, and retry if it fails.

58
00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:44.962
And the cost.

59
00:03:44.962 --> 00:03:54.884
A change that used to be one file is now several releases, a backfill that may run for hours, and a team that knows what each version may touch.

60
00:03:54.884 --> 00:03:57.847
Nothing in the database enforces the order.

61
00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:01.230
So, how do you change a column under running code?

62
00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.116
Like a bridge.

63
00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:07.367
Build the new one beside the old, move the traffic, and take the old one down last.

64
00:04:07.833 --> 00:04:08.957
One quick check.

65
00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:14.786
A teammate's version 4 stops writing amount, and the same release drops the column.

66
00:04:14.786 --> 00:04:15.910
What goes wrong?

67
00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.033
Pause and think.

68
00:04:20.233 --> 00:04:23.061
The drop runs when the first version 4 starts.

69
00:04:23.061 --> 00:04:30.067
The others are still version 3, and still write amount, so every one of their writes fails until the rollout ends.

70
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:33.693
And there's no going back to version 3: its column is gone.
